**6. Write the function *mean_ci()* that is provided a numeric vector and a level of confidence (with a default of 0.95) and returns a list that includes the sample size, the sample mean, the sample standard deviation, the standard error, and a two-item named vector with the lower and upper bounds of the confidence interval. Demonstrate this function (a) by letting R print out the list and (b) by storing the list and then using it to print out only the confidence interval rounded to two decimal places. For your demonstration, use 200 numbers drawn at random from a normal distribution. Also report on whether the confidence interval included the true parameter or resulted in a Type I error. Use the *set.seed()* function so that your report of results will stay constant.**

```{r}

# Define the function.

mean_ci <- function(x, conf_level = 0.95) {
  
  # Ensure x is numeric.
  
  if (!is.numeric(x)) {
    return("Error: Input must be a numeric vector.")
  }
  
  # Sample size.
  
  n <- length(x)
  
  # Sample mean.
  
  mean_x <- mean(x)
  
  # Sample standard deviation.
  
  sd_x <- sd(x)
  
  # Standard Error.
  
  se_x <- sd_x / sqrt(n)
  
  # Critical t-value for confidence interval.
  
  t_value <- qt((1 + conf_level) / 2, df = n - 1)
  
  # Compute confidence interval bounds.
  
  ci_lower <- mean_x - t_value * se_x
  ci_upper <- mean_x + t_value * se_x
  
  # Return results as a list.
  
  return(list(
    Sample_Size = n,
    Mean = mean_x,
    SD = sd_x,
    SE = se_x,
    CI = c(Lower_Bound = ci_lower, Upper_Bound = ci_upper)
  ))
}

# Set seed.

set.seed(123)

# Generate 200 random numbers from a normal distribution.

random_sample <- rnorm(200, mean = 100, sd = 15)

# Print the full list from the function.

ci_results <- mean_ci(random_sample)
print(ci_results)


```
```{r}

# Extract and display only the confidence interval.

ci_bounds <- round(ci_results$CI, 2)
print(ci_bounds)


```


```{r}

# True population mean (100) is within the confidence interval.

